course: dictionary
lesson: 8676
title: Sediment: what settles
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Sediment is sand, mud, stones and other small bits that water, wind or ice carry along, and then drop in a layer when they slow down or stop. «A delta forms where a river drops its sediment at the mouth faster than the sea can carry it away.» The clues are drops and forms: the river leaves its load behind, and over time that load builds up into new land.
explain: Look at the word's movement. «The world's rivers carry billions of tonnes of sediment to the sea every year.» Carry tells you the sediment is moving, and sediment settling tells you it stops somewhere, usually at the bottom of a river, lake or sea.
explain: You can see sediment in your own kitchen. Stir muddy water, leave it to stand, and a layer forms at the bottom. That layer is sediment. The same thing can happen in a glass of juice or a bottle of medicine.
fact: Sediment is sand, mud, stones and other bits carried by water, wind or ice.
fact: Sediment settles in a layer, for example at the bottom of a river, lake or sea.
fact: Rivers drop sediment where they slow down, forming deltas, beaches and floodplains.
fact: Over millions of years, layers of sediment press together into sedimentary rock, such as sandstone, chalk and limestone.
fact: Fossils are often found in sedimentary rock.
exercise: «A delta forms where a river drops its sediment at the mouth.» What does sediment mean here? | A type of fish that lives in deltas | *Sand, mud and stones that a river carried and dropped | Hot air rising above the river | Salty sea water
exercise: Which of these would settle as sediment? | Bubbles rising to the top of a fizzy drink | Sunlight shining on the water | *Sand and mud at the bottom of a lake | A kite flying high on a windy day
exercise: Layers of sediment press together over millions of years to form what? | *Sedimentary rock, such as sandstone or chalk | Fresh water from the sea | Ice cubes | Floating islands
test: «The world's rivers carry billions of tonnes of sediment to the sea every year.» What does the word carry tell you about sediment? | *It is moved along by the water | It is a kind of plant that grows in rivers | It stays still on the land forever | It is always red
test: You leave muddy water to stand, and a layer forms at the bottom. What is that layer? | Vinegar | A bubble | Steam | *Sediment
exam: Which sentence uses sediment correctly? | Sediment is a loud noise made by the sea | The sediment sang a happy song | We ate sediment for lunch | *The river dropped layers of sediment at its mouth, where it slowed down
